IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `intl-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for International Enforcement and Cooperation (2017), chapter 5:
-- international cooperation.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. Chapter 5 "focuses on investigations and case
-- cooperation" (5 [pdf page 40]). Encoded are the sentences that say what the agencies or the
-- Department may, will, will not or cannot do, each with its own modal: cooperation by the
-- state of the investigations (5, n.138), compulsory process (5.1.1), the IAEAA and HSR
-- materials (2.6, 5.1.3), the kinds of information shared and waivers (5.1.4), remedies
-- reaching abroad (5.1.5), and grand jury secrecy and leniency (5.2). The rest of chapter 5
-- describes practice ("can", "often", "generally provide" [pdf page 52]) and is inert (fork F16; NOTES.md
-- section 6).
-- 5.1.2 summarises the confidentiality statutes and is inert; its one bearing here is
-- 5.1.4's "confidentiality obligations generally prohibit" [pdf page 47], which cites it (n.162).

§ `Chapter 5: international cooperation`

§§ `5 Cooperation by the state of the investigations`

DECLARE `What chapter 5 says of cooperation` IS ONE OF
    `the Agency may consult with the relevant foreign authorities about the matter, and coordinate and cooperate with those authorities conducting parallel investigations`
    `an Agency may continue that cooperation when either it or the foreign authority has closed its investigation`
    `the agencies may engage in general discussions with foreign authorities on matters in which only one authority has an open investigation`

-- 5 [pdf page 41]: "When either Agency reviews a case that raises possible competitive
-- concerns in jurisdictions outside of the United States, it may consult with the relevant
-- foreign authorities about the matter and coordinate and cooperate with those authorities
-- conducting parallel investigations." n.138 [pdf page 41] adds the other two states. All
-- three are "may". n.139's statement that the agencies "do not conduct 'joint
-- investigations'" is a description of practice and is inert.
@ref 5 [pdf pages 40-41]; n.138, n.139 [pdf page 41]
GIVEN s IS A `State of the investigations (5)`
GIVETH A `What chapter 5 says of cooperation`
`5: cooperation with the foreign authority` s MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF s EQUALS `the Agency and the foreign authority are conducting parallel investigations of the matter`
      THEN `the Agency may consult with the relevant foreign authorities about the matter, and coordinate and cooperate with those authorities conducting parallel investigations`
      IF s EQUALS `the Agency or the foreign authority has closed its investigation, after they cooperated`
      THEN `an Agency may continue that cooperation when either it or the foreign authority has closed its investigation`
      OTHERWISE `the agencies may engage in general discussions with foreign authorities on matters in which only one authority has an open investigation`

§§ `5.1.1 Investigative tools`

DECLARE `What section 5.1.1 says of compelled production` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may compel the production of the documents or information, including documents or information located outside the United States`

-- 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]: "The Agencies may compel the production of documents or information,
-- including documents or information located outside the United States, when the documents
-- or information sought are within the 'possession, custody, or control' of an individual or
-- entity subject to the jurisdiction of the United States and are not protected by the
-- attorney-client privilege or the work-product doctrine." The sentence says "when" and not
-- only when: if either condition fails, it states nothing (NOTHING; fork F15). The location field is
-- read by no rule. 5.1.1's voluntary requests and the considerations before compulsory
-- measures are used ([pdf page 43]) are inert.
@ref 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]; n.140 to n.142 [pdf page 42]
GIVEN d IS A `Documents or information sought (5.1.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.1.1 says of compelled production`
`5.1.1: whether the agencies may compel production` MEANS
    IF     d's `they are within the possession, custody, or control of an individual or entity subject to the jurisdiction of the United States`
       AND (NOT d's `they are protected by the attorney-client privilege or the work-product doctrine`)
    THEN JUST `the agencies may compel the production of the documents or information, including documents or information located outside the United States`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 5.1.1 says of a foreign blocking statute` IS ONE OF
    `the mere existence of the foreign statute does not excuse noncompliance with a request for documents or information from one of the agencies`

-- 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]: "Conflicts can arise where foreign statutes purport to prevent
-- individuals or entities from disclosing documents or information for use in U.S.
-- proceedings. The mere existence of such statutes, however, does not excuse noncompliance
-- with a request for documents or information from one of the Agencies." The comity side of
-- the same statute, n.144, is in intl-comity.l4.
@ref 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]; n.144 [pdf pages 42-43]
GIVEN d IS A `Documents or information sought (5.1.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.1.1 says of a foreign blocking statute`
`5.1.1: what the mere existence of a blocking statute does` MEANS
    IF d's `a foreign statute purports to prevent disclosing them for use in U.S. proceedings`
    THEN JUST `the mere existence of the foreign statute does not excuse noncompliance with a request for documents or information from one of the agencies`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 5.1.1 says of a Second Request` IS ONE OF
    `compliance with a Second Request requires production of all responsive documents and information, no matter where located`

-- 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]: "Compliance with a Second Request requires production of all
-- responsive documents and information, no matter where located." n.143 cites "Section 2.2.4,
-- supra" for the HSR Act, which is section 2.4 (a slip in the text; NOTES.md section 7).
@ref 5.1.1 [pdf page 42]; n.143 [pdf page 42]
GIVEN d IS A `Documents or information sought (5.1.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.1.1 says of a Second Request`
`5.1.1: what compliance with a Second Request requires` MEANS
    IF d's `they are sought by a Second Request in a transaction notified under the HSR Act`
    THEN JUST `compliance with a Second Request requires production of all responsive documents and information, no matter where located`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `5.1.4 Types of information exchanged and waivers of confidentiality`

DECLARE `What section 5.1.4 says of sharing the information` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may share relevant publicly available information with the foreign authority`
    `it remains in the agencies' discretion whether to share agency non-public information with the cooperating foreign authority`
    `not before the agencies have reached an understanding that the foreign authority will maintain the agency non-public information in confidence and in accordance with its laws and rules`
    `the agencies will provide the information to the foreign authority pursuant to the waiver`
    `under an IAEAA agreement, the agencies may share evidence relating to antitrust violations already in their possession, including statutorily protected confidential information, subject to certain limitations`
    `the IAEAA does not apply to materials submitted pursuant to the HSR Act, and confidentiality obligations generally prohibit the agencies from disclosing the information to the foreign authority`
    `confidentiality obligations generally prohibit the agencies from disclosing to foreign authorities confidential information submitted by a person`

-- 5.1.4 [pdf pages 47-48], 2.6 [pdf page 13], 5.1.3 [pdf page 46]. The paragraph opens "If a
-- transaction or conduct under antitrust investigation in the United States is also being
-- investigated by a foreign authority": otherwise NOTHING. Then, by kind of information:
--   public: "The Agencies may share with these foreign authorities relevant publicly
--     available information" (n.160);
--   agency non-public: "it remains in the Agencies' discretion whether to share"; "Before
--     exchanging agency non-public information, the Agencies will have reached an
--     understanding that the foreign authority will maintain the information in confidence";
--   "declining to grant a waiver will not preclude the Agencies from sharing publicly
--     available or agency non-public information", so neither of those reads the waiver;
--   confidential information: "the Agencies will provide information to foreign authorities
--     pursuant to a waiver when they have reached an understanding with the recipient
--     agency"; under an IAEAA agreement the authorities "may share evidence ... including
--     statutorily protected confidential information" (2.6), "subject to certain
--     limitations" (5.1.3), but "the IAEAA does not apply to materials submitted pursuant to
--     the HSR Act" (2.6, repeated at 5.1.3); otherwise "confidentiality obligations generally
--     prohibit the Agencies from disclosing to foreign authorities confidential information
--     submitted by a person".
-- A covering waiver with no understanding and no IAEAA route is NOTHING: the waiver lifts
-- the general prohibition, and the text says what the agencies will do only "when they have
-- reached an understanding" (fork F18). Grand jury and leniency information is chapter 5.2's
-- (`5.2: sharing criminal investigation information`), so NOTHING here.
@ref 5.1.4 [pdf pages 47-48]; 2.6 [pdf page 13]; 5.1.3 [pdf page 46]; n.47, n.159, n.160 to n.162
GIVEN i IS A `Information for a foreign authority (5.1.4)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.1.4 says of sharing the information`
`5.1.4: sharing the information with the foreign authority` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT i's `the foreign authority is also investigating the transaction or conduct`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF i's `the kind of information` EQUALS `relevant publicly available information`
      THEN JUST `the agencies may share relevant publicly available information with the foreign authority`
      IF i's `the kind of information` EQUALS `agency non-public information`
      THEN IF i's `the agencies have reached an understanding that the foreign authority will maintain the information in confidence, consistent with its laws and rules`
           THEN JUST `it remains in the agencies' discretion whether to share agency non-public information with the cooperating foreign authority`
           ELSE JUST `not before the agencies have reached an understanding that the foreign authority will maintain the agency non-public information in confidence and in accordance with its laws and rules`
      IF NOT (i's `the kind of information` EQUALS `confidential information submitted by a person`)
      THEN NOTHING
      IF     i's `the waiver` EQUALS `the person has granted a waiver for this matter that designates the foreign authority`
         AND i's `the agencies have reached an understanding that the foreign authority will maintain the information in confidence, consistent with its laws and rules`
      THEN JUST `the agencies will provide the information to the foreign authority pursuant to the waiver`
      IF     i's `an IAEAA mutual assistance agreement is in force between the United States and the foreign authority`
         AND (NOT i's `the information was submitted pursuant to the HSR Act`)
      THEN JUST `under an IAEAA agreement, the agencies may share evidence relating to antitrust violations already in their possession, including statutorily protected confidential information, subject to certain limitations`
      IF i's `the waiver` EQUALS `the person has granted a waiver for this matter that designates the foreign authority`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF     i's `an IAEAA mutual assistance agreement is in force between the United States and the foreign authority`
         AND i's `the information was submitted pursuant to the HSR Act`
      THEN JUST `the IAEAA does not apply to materials submitted pursuant to the HSR Act, and confidentiality obligations generally prohibit the agencies from disclosing the information to the foreign authority`
      OTHERWISE JUST `confidentiality obligations generally prohibit the agencies from disclosing to foreign authorities confidential information submitted by a person`

DECLARE `What section 5.1.4 says of refusing a waiver` IS ONE OF
    `refusal to provide a waiver will not prejudice the outcome of an investigation, though in some cases the absence of a waiver may have practical effects such as increasing the risk of inconsistent outcomes between jurisdictions`

-- 5.1.4 [pdf page 48]: "The Agencies may request a waiver of confidentiality, but the
-- decision whether to provide one rests solely with the producing person. Refusal to provide
-- a waiver will not prejudice the outcome of an investigation, though, in some cases, the
-- absence of a waiver may have practical effects". Read for a refusal only: a waiver never
-- asked for is NOTHING (fork F17).
@ref 5.1.4 [pdf page 48]
GIVEN w IS A `Waiver of confidentiality (5.1.4)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.1.4 says of refusing a waiver`
`5.1.4: what refusing a waiver does to the investigation` w MEANS
    IF w EQUALS `the person has declined to provide a waiver`
    THEN JUST `refusal to provide a waiver will not prejudice the outcome of an investigation, though in some cases the absence of a waiver may have practical effects such as increasing the risk of inconsistent outcomes between jurisdictions`
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 5.1.4 says of information from a foreign authority` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will not seek information that is privileged under U.S. law from foreign authorities through waivers or other cooperative activities`
    `the agencies will protect information received from a foreign authority pursuant to a waiver under applicable provisions of U.S. law`

-- 5.1.4 [pdf page 48]: "The Agencies will protect information received from a foreign
-- authority pursuant to a waiver under applicable provisions of U.S. law. The Agencies will
-- not seek information that is privileged under U.S. law from foreign authorities through
-- waivers or other cooperative activities." Both can hold of one item; each is listed.
@ref 5.1.4 [pdf page 48]; n.165 [pdf page 48]
GIVEN f IS A `Information from a foreign authority (5.1.4)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What section 5.1.4 says of information from a foreign authority`
`5.1.4: what the agencies say of information from a foreign authority` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF f's `it is privileged under U.S. law` THEN JUST `the agencies will not seek information that is privileged under U.S. law from foreign authorities through waivers or other cooperative activities` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF f's `the agencies received it from the foreign authority pursuant to a waiver` THEN JUST `the agencies will protect information received from a foreign authority pursuant to a waiver under applicable provisions of U.S. law` ELSE NOTHING))

§§ `5.1.5 Remedies`

DECLARE `What section 5.1.5 says of a remedy reaching outside the United States` IS ONE OF
    `the Agency will not seek to include the conduct or assets outside the United States: including them is not needed to effectively redress harm or threatened harm to U.S. commerce and consumers`
    `the Agency will not seek to include the conduct or assets outside the United States: including them is not consistent with the Agency's international comity analysis`
    `including the conduct or assets outside the United States is within the extent to which an Agency will seek such a remedy`

-- 5.1.5 [pdf page 50]: "An Agency will seek a remedy that includes conduct or assets outside
-- the United States only to the extent that including them is needed to effectively redress
-- harm or threatened harm to U.S. commerce and consumers and is consistent with the Agency's
-- international comity analysis." "Only to the extent" is a limit: when either finding
-- fails, the text's no; when both hold, the limit does not bar it, which is all the sentence
-- says (the third result does not say the Agency will seek it). Need is read first.
@ref 5.1.5 [pdf page 50]; n.166 to n.168 [pdf page 50]
GIVEN r IS A `Remedy reaching outside the United States (5.1.5)`
GIVETH A `What section 5.1.5 says of a remedy reaching outside the United States`
`5.1.5: a remedy that includes conduct or assets outside the United States` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT r's `including them is needed to effectively redress harm or threatened harm to U.S. commerce and consumers`
      THEN `the Agency will not seek to include the conduct or assets outside the United States: including them is not needed to effectively redress harm or threatened harm to U.S. commerce and consumers`
      IF NOT r's `including them is consistent with the Agency's international comity analysis`
      THEN `the Agency will not seek to include the conduct or assets outside the United States: including them is not consistent with the Agency's international comity analysis`
      OTHERWISE `including the conduct or assets outside the United States is within the extent to which an Agency will seek such a remedy`

§§ `5.2 Special considerations in criminal investigations`

DECLARE `What section 5.2 says of sharing criminal investigation information` IS ONE OF
    `the Department is prohibited from disclosing matters occurring before the grand jury absent an applicable exception, and the prohibition cannot be waived by a subject of the investigation, a grand jury witness, or a recipient of a grand jury subpoena`
    `the grand jury secrecy prohibition does not apply to these persons, and therefore does not generally prohibit disclosures by them`
    `the applicant's waiver allows the Department to share the applicant's identity and information with the foreign authority`
    `the Department holds the identity of leniency applicants and the information they provide in strict confidence`

-- 5.2 [pdf pages 53-54]: "The Department is prohibited, however, from disclosing matters
-- occurring before the grand jury absent an applicable exception. This prohibition cannot be
-- waived by a subject of the investigation, a grand jury witness, or a recipient of a grand
-- jury subpoena. The prohibition, however, does not apply to these persons and therefore does
-- not generally prohibit disclosures by them." So for the Department a waiver changes
-- nothing; with an applicable exception the text states nothing more (NOTHING, since the
-- exceptions are not described; fork F27). On leniency: "The Department holds the identity of leniency
-- applicants and the information they provide in strict confidence"; "A leniency applicant
-- can agree to waive this confidentiality assurance and allow the Department to share the
-- applicant's identity and information with a foreign authority." Other kinds of information
-- are 5.1.4's, so NOTHING here.
@ref 5.2 [pdf pages 53-54]; n.173, n.174 [pdf page 53]
GIVEN c IS A `Criminal investigation information for a foreign authority (5.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.2 says of sharing criminal investigation information`
`5.2: sharing criminal investigation information` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF c's `the kind of information` EQUALS `matters occurring before the grand jury`
      THEN IF c's `the person disclosing` EQUALS `a subject of the investigation, a grand jury witness, or a recipient of a grand jury subpoena`
           THEN JUST `the grand jury secrecy prohibition does not apply to these persons, and therefore does not generally prohibit disclosures by them`
           ELSE IF c's `an exception to the grand jury secrecy rule applies`
                THEN NOTHING
                ELSE JUST `the Department is prohibited from disclosing matters occurring before the grand jury absent an applicable exception, and the prohibition cannot be waived by a subject of the investigation, a grand jury witness, or a recipient of a grand jury subpoena`
      IF c's `the kind of information` EQUALS `the identity of a leniency applicant, or information the applicant provided`
      THEN IF c's `the waiver` EQUALS `the person has granted a waiver for this matter that designates the foreign authority`
           THEN JUST `the applicant's waiver allows the Department to share the applicant's identity and information with the foreign authority`
           ELSE JUST `the Department holds the identity of leniency applicants and the information they provide in strict confidence`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

DECLARE `What section 5.2 says of a leniency applicant's materials` IS ONE OF
    `the applicant's required cooperation includes producing them, wherever located`

-- 5.2 [pdf page 53]: "That required cooperation includes the production of all documents,
-- information, or other materials in the applicant's possession, custody, or control,
-- wherever located, that are requested by the Department in connection with the criminal
-- antitrust investigation and are not protected by the attorney-client privilege or the
-- work-product doctrine." "Includes": when a condition fails, the sentence states nothing
-- (NOTHING). The location field is read by no rule.
@ref 5.2 [pdf page 53]; n.175 [pdf page 53]
GIVEN m IS A `Materials requested of a leniency applicant (5.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 5.2 says of a leniency applicant's materials`
`5.2: a leniency applicant's materials` MEANS
    IF     m's `they are in the applicant's possession, custody, or control`
       AND m's `they are requested by the Department in connection with the criminal antitrust investigation`
       AND (NOT m's `they are protected by the attorney-client privilege or the work-product doctrine`)
    THEN JUST `the applicant's required cooperation includes producing them, wherever located`
    ELSE NOTHING
